//? Title: Bisection: Brackets, Residuals and Honest Stopping Rules
//? Subtitle: The square-root example, exact halving, function scaling and finite-precision limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a continuous function with opposite signs at two endpoints, locate a zero
while retaining a bracket around at least one root. Halving provides a positional
error bound, whereas a small function value alone generally does not. We work a
three-step linear example, retain the original square-root calculation, and
check the recorded bracket widths against the halving formula. Separate
controls expose residual stopping, discontinuities, invalid callbacks, finite
iteration budgets and exhausted floating-point precision.
)

\toc

> The method

Assume f is continuous on \([a,b]\), with \(a<b\), finite evaluated values,
and opposite nonzero endpoint signs. The intermediate value theorem gives a
root in that interval. Evaluate its midpoint m. If \(f(m)=0\), stop; otherwise
retain the half whose endpoints have opposite signs. Continuity preserves the
root enclosure, and each nonterminal update halves the interval.

The core compares signs directly, without multiplying callback values. It uses
an overflow-safe midpoint rather than forming a+b. The recorded radius is the
rounded maximum of the distances from that midpoint to the two endpoints;
this accommodates a floating midpoint that is not exactly central.

In exact arithmetic, for the kth midpoint evaluation, starting at k=1,

$$
L_k=\frac{b_0-a_0}{2^{k-1}},\qquad
|m_k-r|\le\frac{L_k}{2}=\frac{b_0-a_0}{2^k}
$$
\alttext(The bracket before midpoint evaluation k has width equal to the initial width divided by two to k minus one. Its midpoint is at most half that width from an enclosed root.)

for at least one enclosed root r. Thus reaching a positional tolerance epsilon
requires a number of evaluations growing logarithmically with the initial width
divided by epsilon. The interval shrinks geometrically by one binary digit per
step. This does not assert that the actual midpoint error decreases monotonically.

> Simple example: three midpoint evaluations

For \(f(x)=x-3/4\) on \([0,2]\), the midpoint 1 has a positive value, so
retain \([0,1]\). Midpoint 1/2 is negative, so retain \([1/2,1]\). The next
midpoint 3/4 returns zero. These dyadic values are exactly representable.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bisection_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bisection_trace([](double x){return x-.75;},0,2);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  std::print("| evaluation | lower | upper | midpoint | f(midpoint) | radius |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.steps.size();++i) {
    const auto& step=trace.steps[i];
    diagram.node(std::to_string(i),{6.*i,0},std::format("Bracket [{}, {}]\nm = {}, f(m) = {}",
        step.lower,step.upper,step.midpoint,step.f_midpoint));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i+1,step.lower,step.upper,
               step.midpoint,step.f_midpoint,step.radius);
  }
  diagram.edge("0","1","keep left").edge("1","2","keep right");
  diagram.save_svg("build/bisection_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| evaluation | lower | upper | midpoint | f(midpoint) | radius |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0 | 2 | 1 | 0.25 | 1 |
| 2 | 0 | 1 | 0.5 | -0.25 | 0.5 |
| 3 | 0.5 | 1 | 0.75 | 0 | 0.25 |
\image(figures/bisection_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The core records each bracket before its midpoint test. A zero callback ends the third evaluation without another bracket update.)
\alttext(Three boxes show brackets zero to two, zero to one, and one half to one. Their midpoints are one, one half and three quarters.)

> The original example: square root of two

Let \(f(x)=x^2-2\) on \([1,2]\). The first midpoints are
\(1.5,1.25,1.375,1.4375,\ldots\). The trace uses only the position test by
default. The historical `bisection(f,a,b,tol,max_iter)` interface remains
available and applies the same tol to both the residual and interval tests.
Both interfaces use the same underlying loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bisection_sqrt_two.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto f=[](double x){return x*x-2;};
  const auto original=dm::algorithms::bisection(f,1,2);
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bisection_trace(f,1,2,{1e-10,0,200});
  std::print("| step | lower | upper | midpoint | radius |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> first,lower,upper,midpoint,all,measured,predicted;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.steps.size();++i) {
    const auto& s=trace.steps[i];
    if (i<8) std::print("| {} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n",i+1,s.lower,s.upper,s.midpoint,s.radius);
    if (i<10) { first.push_back(i+1);lower.push_back(s.lower);upper.push_back(s.upper);midpoint.push_back(s.midpoint); }
    all.push_back(i+1);measured.push_back(-std::log2(s.radius));predicted.push_back(i+1);
  }
  std::print("\nHistorical interface: root {:.12f}, residual {:.3e}, {} iterations.\n"
             "Position-only trace: root {:.12f}, radius {:.3e}, {} iterations, {} callback calls, stop = {}.\n",
             original.root,original.residual,original.iterations,trace.result.root,trace.radius,
             trace.result.iterations,trace.evaluations,dm::algorithms::bisection_stop_name(trace.stop));
  auto bracket=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  bracket.lines(first,lower,"lower",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
         .lines(first,upper,"upper",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
         .points(first,midpoint,"midpoint",dm::RGB{17,24,39},5)
         .title("First ten recorded brackets").x_label("midpoint evaluation").y_label("coordinate")
         .y_limits(.95,2.2);
  auto rate=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  rate.lines(all,predicted,"exact halving",dm::RGB{148,163,184},3)
      .points(all,measured,"recorded radius",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .title("One binary digit per evaluation").x_label("midpoint evaluation").y_label("negative log2(radius)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(bracket).add(rate).size(1100,480).fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/bisection_sqrt_two.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| step | lower | upper | midpoint | radius |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1.00000000 | 2.00000000 | 1.50000000 | 0.50000000 |
| 2 | 1.00000000 | 1.50000000 | 1.25000000 | 0.25000000 |
| 3 | 1.25000000 | 1.50000000 | 1.37500000 | 0.12500000 |
| 4 | 1.37500000 | 1.50000000 | 1.43750000 | 0.06250000 |
| 5 | 1.37500000 | 1.43750000 | 1.40625000 | 0.03125000 |
| 6 | 1.40625000 | 1.43750000 | 1.42187500 | 0.01562500 |
| 7 | 1.40625000 | 1.42187500 | 1.41406250 | 0.00781250 |
| 8 | 1.41406250 | 1.42187500 | 1.41796875 | 0.00390625 |

Historical interface: root 1.414213562384, residual 3.154e-11, 29 iterations.
Position-only trace: root 1.414213562326, radius 5.821e-11, 34 iterations, 36 callback calls, stop = interval.
\image(figures/bisection_sqrt_two.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The left panel retains the original shrinking-bracket experiment. The right checks the exact-arithmetic radius formula on this representable dyadic sequence; all iterations, rather than only the first ten, appear there.)
\alttext(The bracket endpoints approach square root of two. A second panel shows the negative binary logarithm of the radius increasing by exactly one per midpoint evaluation.)

The kth recorded radius is \(2^{-k}\) in this example. The strict position
test is radius less than the requested tolerance; equality does not yet stop.
Midpoint evaluation counts exclude the initial endpoint calls, which the trace
reports separately. An endpoint callback zero needs no midpoint iteration.

> A small residual does not bound coordinate error

Rescale the simple linear function to \(g(x)=10^{-20}(x-3/4)\). Its root
has not moved. At the initial midpoint 1, however, the residual is
\(2.5\times10^{-21}\), already below the historical tolerance \(10^{-10}\),
even though the coordinate error is 1/4.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto f=[](double x){return 1e-20*(x-.75);};
  const auto combined=dm::algorithms::bisection_trace(f,0,2,{1e-10,1e-10,200});
  const auto position=dm::algorithms::bisection_trace(f,0,2,{1e-10,0,200});
  std::print("| stopping controls | estimate | actual coordinate error | residual | bracket radius | reason |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& [label,trace]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,dm::algorithms::BisectionTrace>>{
       {"historical combined tolerance",combined},{"position test only",position}})
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.3e} | {} | {} |\n",label,trace.result.root,
        std::abs(trace.result.root-.75),trace.result.residual,trace.radius,
        dm::algorithms::bisection_stop_name(trace.stop));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| stopping controls | estimate | actual coordinate error | residual | bracket radius | reason |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| historical combined tolerance | 1 | 0.25 | 2.500e-21 | 1 | residual |
| position test only | 0.75 | 0 | 0.000e+00 | 0.25 | zero callback |
// result_end
\caption(The same root and interval give different stopping behavior after merely rescaling function units. A zero residual tolerance disables that test, while callback zeros still stop.)

A derivative lower bound can connect residual to position: if f is differentiable
and \(|f'|\ge m>0\) between the estimate and a root, the mean value theorem gives
\(|x-r|\le |f(x)|/m\). Without such information, a residual tolerance is a
condition on function units rather than coordinate accuracy.

> Checking the assumptions and failure modes

A finite jump can keep opposite endpoint signs without a root. The function
that returns -1 below 0.3 and +1 elsewhere never returns zero, yet its brackets
shrink to the jump. The interval stopping criterion can pass with residual one.
Continuity is an assumption; the numerical routine cannot establish it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const double adjacent=std::nextafter(1.,2.);
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string,dm::algorithms::BisectionTrace>> trials{
    {"no sign change",dm::algorithms::bisection_trace([](double x){return x*x+1;},-1,1)},
    {"finite jump",dm::algorithms::bisection_trace([](double x){return x<.3?-1.:1.;},0,1,{1e-8,0,200})},
    {"two-iteration budget",dm::algorithms::bisection_trace([](double x){return x*x-2;},1,2,{1e-30,0,2})},
    {"adjacent endpoints",dm::algorithms::bisection_trace([](double x){return x==1?-1.:1.;},1,adjacent,
        {std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min(),0,200})},
    {"large finite coordinates",dm::algorithms::bisection_trace([](double x){return x-1.4e308;},1e308,1.6e308,{1e292,0,200})}};
  std::print("| case | reason | stopping criterion passed | midpoint evaluations | residual |\n"
             "| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [label,trace]:trials) {
    if (trace.stop==dm::algorithms::BisectionStop::NoBracket)
      std::print("| {} | {} | false | 0 | — |\n",label,dm::algorithms::bisection_stop_name(trace.stop));
    else std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.3e} |\n",label,
        dm::algorithms::bisection_stop_name(trace.stop),trace.result.converged,
        trace.result.iterations,trace.result.residual);
  }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::bisection_trace([](double x){return 1/x;},-1,1); }
  catch (const std::domain_error& error) { std::print("\nPole callback: {}.\n",error.what()); }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| case | reason | stopping criterion passed | midpoint evaluations | residual |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |
| no sign change | no bracket | false | 0 | — |
| finite jump | interval | true | 27 | 1.000e+00 |
| two-iteration budget | iteration limit | false | 2 | 4.375e-01 |
| adjacent endpoints | stagnation | false | 1 | 1.000e+00 |
| large finite coordinates | zero callback | true | 52 | 0.000e+00 |

Pole callback: bisection: callback returned a nonfinite value.
// result_end
\caption(No-bracket, exhausted budget and exhausted precision are distinct outcomes. The finite jump passes a numerical interval test without satisfying the theorem's continuity assumption.)

The maximum recorded midpoint-to-endpoint distance is a rounded quantity, not
an outward-rounded interval-arithmetic certificate. A midpoint can round to an
endpoint when no representable interior value remains. If neither tolerance
has passed, the solver returns `stagnation` instead of repeating that endpoint.
An overflow-safe midpoint handles large finite endpoints; nonfinite callback
values, including an evaluated pole, raise a domain error. A callback's returned
zero may itself reflect rounding or underflow, so even that reason is named
`zero callback`, rather than a mathematical proof of an exact root.

> Conclusion

The original square-root example verifies the bracket contraction rate, and the
scaled linear control separates residual stopping from coordinate accuracy.
The trace exposes endpoint calls, midpoint counts, terminal brackets and actual
stopping reasons. Under continuity and trustworthy evaluations, interval halving
provides the positional argument; finite precision, callback errors and missing
continuity remain explicit limits. These analytic controls need no observed dataset.

The unrecorded interface retains constant auxiliary storage and the historical
combined tolerance. Recording uses one owned snapshot per midpoint evaluation,
with independent function- and coordinate-unit tolerances.

> References

- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, section 3.8(iii), *Bisection Method*. https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.8#iii
